      "id": 1,
      "text": "Harrisburg was platted in 1871 along a planned Louisiana and Missouri River Railroad line that never reached the town, stunting its growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        5,
        6,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harrisburg%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors John Woods Harris, an early county leader who owned the Model Farm on Thrall’s Prairie.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harrisburg%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1878, the Ancient Landmark Masonic Lodge #356 built a two‑story brick building that served as Harrisburg’s school and community center until 1963.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/harrisburg_missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Harrisburg sits on a high point between Moniteau Creek and Perche Creek, at the headwaters of Callahan Creek.",
